{
  "dom_id": "step-q217277",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The solution to $\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{6}{y}$ is$\\,\\,y\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{6}{y} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{-6}{y} \\\\ 3\\cdot y & =2\\cdot (-6) \\\\ 3y & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y & =-12 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-12}{3} \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y=-4$ is different from $y=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $y=-4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
